(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).afoqt=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.afoqt.full||[]).push({
  num: 1, part: `Part One: The Posting`, title: `Engine Run`, weeks: `January 9 to 10, 2027`,
  tasks: [`SJ`,`VA`,`WK`,`AI`,`TR`,`AR`],
  cast: [
    [`Kemp`,`Staff sergeant. Crew chief on the jet parked next to two two eight. Talks in the break room.`]
  ],
  opening: [
    `At 4:10 on a Saturday morning in January, Kit Darrow sat in the left seat of a jet that wasn't allowed to go anywhere.`,
    `Two two eight was chocked front and back, brake set, throttles up at run power, and every rivet in her was shoving at chocks that weren't going anywhere either. Kit could feel it in her fillings. Out front the north ramp at Tolliver Field was black and wet. The Guard hangars were lit up orange, and past them was the Missouri River, which at that hour you mostly knew was there because it was darker than everything else. Eight degrees outside. The heater was losing.`,
    `Night shift had changed a fuel pump on number three, and this run was the leak check. It was on the schedule at four so it would be done before the morning launch, and before half of Tolliver woke up and called the airport about the noise. Fisk was out by the nose on a long headset cord, watching for leaks, a reflective vest and a flashlight and not much else.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Brisk, eager, a little proud.">"Three's dry, Sergeant. No leaks. I looked twice."</span>`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Calm, clipped, practiced.">"Copy. Bringing them back."</span>`,
    `She pulled the throttles back. The jet settled onto its gear like a kid who'd been told no. She ran the shutdown checklist, then stayed put after the last engine quit, listening to the gyros wind down and the metal tick.`,
    `Engine run qualified meant she could start the engines, run them, check them, and shut them down. It didn't mean she could let off the brakes. In eleven years on the flight line this was the only seat she'd ever had up front: the left one, in a jet that was chocked. Her name was stenciled under the pilot's window outside, CREW CHIEF SSGT K. DARROW, white block letters Boyd had painted in 2018 over the place where his own name had been.`,
    `Behind the pilots, where the navigator used to sit, there was nothing now but a row of bolt holes in the floor. She'd sat in a seat like that once, at seven, on a family day, in one of the wing's jets. Which jet, she couldn't have told you. A man's voice. His hand pointing up at the ceiling, and her staring hard at the spot, trying to see what he saw. Wanting something so badly she'd cried in the car on the way home.`,
    `At 4:50 she walked into the shop. Somebody had made the coffee too strong, which was the only way the shop made coffee, and on the corkboard next to the pot, pinned over a notice about a lost pair of gloves, was a new sheet of paper.`,
    `<prop type="doc" title="Pilot vacancy, summer 2027 hiring board">The wing will select one applicant for undergraduate pilot training. The selection board convenes Saturday, July 10, 2027.<br>Applicants must hold a bachelor's degree, meet commissioning standards, and have AFOQT scores on file by June 1, 2027. Minimum scores are on page two.<br>Applicants must not have reached their 33rd birthday by the board date.<br>Selection is contingent on a flight physical and on final approval above the wing.</prop>`,
    `She read it twice. Then she read the birthday line a third time, pulled a blank work card out of the binder rack and did the math on the back of it, the math she'd known she would do before she got to it. June 3, thirty three. July 10, the board. Five weeks. Next summer she'd be a whole year further past it.`,
    `Five weeks over a line somebody had typed. She folded the card in half, put it in her chest pocket, and couldn't have said yet whether five weeks was a wall or a crack.`,
    `Seven years ago she'd put in for a board like this one, and that winter she'd pulled her package to stay for the spring inspection, because Boyd had asked her to. That was the story. She'd told it so many times it had worn smooth, like a wrench handle.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Vern's Nav Table: what you're sitting down to`,
    sections: [
      { h: `The test, start to finish`,
        body: [`<!--story--><span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Vern Pruett, lieutenant colonel, retired. One Sunday in January a crew chief named Kit Darrow took the stool next to mine at the Riverside Diner. I'll get to that. Until I do, whenever she makes a call, you'll hear what I'd have told her."</span>`,
          `Most break room talk about this test is wrong. Here are the plain facts.`,
          `It's the Air Force Officer Qualifying Test, the AFOQT. The current version is Form T, in use since 2014. You take it on a computer at a Pearson test center, a base test office, or an ROTC detachment, and you need a voucher from a recruiter or a base test control officer to book it. It's free, and it isn't given at MEPS. Plan on about five hours with breaks.`,
          `There are twelve subtests, and eleven of them count. The current Pearson page lists them in this order, with items and minutes. Verbal Analogies, 25 in 8. Arithmetic Reasoning, 25 in 29. Word Knowledge, 25 in 5. Math Knowledge, 25 in 22. Reading Comprehension, 25 in 24. Situational Judgment, 16 in 35. A fifteen minute break. The Self-Description Inventory, 240 statements in 45 minutes, which isn't scored. Physical Science, 20 in 10. Table Reading, 40 in 7. Instrument Comprehension, 25 in 5. Block Counting, 30 in 5. Aviation Information, 20 in 8.`,
          `Every item has five choices except Instrument Comprehension, which has four. Your score is the number you get right. Nothing comes off for a wrong answer, so never leave one blank. Scores post in two to three business days.`,
          `This course isn't affiliated with or endorsed by the Department of the Air Force, AFPC, or Pearson.`],
        terms: [[`Form T`,`The current version of the AFOQT, in use since 2014 and now given on a computer.`],[`Voucher`,`What a recruiter or base test control officer gives you so you can schedule the test with Pearson.`]],
        exam: `The 2015 paper pamphlet lists different numbers for a few subtests, including 50 Situational Judgment picks. The Pearson page is newer, so this course uses its numbers. Check it again before your date.` },
      { h: `Composites, minimums, and two tries`,
        body: [`Subtest scores roll into composites, each a percentile from 1 to 99. A 60 means you beat about sixty percent of the people it's measured against. Verbal comes from Verbal Analogies, Word Knowledge and Reading Comprehension. Quantitative comes from Arithmetic Reasoning and Math Knowledge. Academic Aptitude uses all five. Pilot leans on Math Knowledge, Table Reading, Instrument Comprehension and Aviation Information. Situational Judgment is reported on its own. Two more rated composites matter if you'd take a seat other than the left one. CSO, for combat systems officers, the job navigators grew into, was Word Knowledge, Math Knowledge, Table Reading and Block Counting in the 2015 pamphlet. ABM, for air battle managers, was Verbal Analogies, Math Knowledge, Table Reading, Instrument Comprehension, Block Counting and Aviation Information. Newer citations list a different mix for both, so treat those two lists as the old ones and check the current version with your unit.`,
          `Any commission needs at least Verbal 15 and Quantitative 10. A pilot applicant needs Pilot 25. Some Guard units have also asked pilot applicants for a CSO score of at least 10, with Pilot plus CSO adding up to 50 or more. Ask your own unit or recruiter what they use. Your board's announcement may set a PCSM minimum too.`,
          `PCSM is the pilot candidate selection method. It combines your Pilot composite, a separate computer test called the TBAS, and your logged flying hours into a score from 1 to 99. The formula isn't published, and since the end of 2021 credit for flying hours stops at 41.`,
          `You get two attempts, at least 90 days apart, and a third takes a waiver. Scores are superscored, so your best result on each composite across your attempts is the one that counts, and Form T scores don't expire. A second try can only raise your best number.`],
        terms: [[`Composite`,`A score built from several subtests and reported as a percentile from 1 to 99.`],[`Superscore`,`Counting your best result on each composite across all your attempts.`],[`PCSM`,`A 1 to 99 score for pilot applicants built from the Pilot composite, the TBAS, and logged flying hours.`]],
        exam: `If you keep one composite in your head, keep Pilot: Math Knowledge, Table Reading, Instrument Comprehension and Aviation Information. Those are the four to push if you want a cockpit.` },
      { h: `Situational Judgment, and why the story works the way it does`,
        body: [`Each Situational Judgment item gives you a problem a junior officer might face and five things you could do about it. You pick the most effective and the least effective. The key comes from experienced officers, and it rewards what works with people, not just what's allowed.`,
          `Handle it directly, privately, and at the lowest level first. Talk to the person before you talk about them. Use the chain of command after a direct try fails, not in place of one. Don't go over a head, don't brief the boss behind a peer's back, and don't quietly do somebody's work for them. Stay honest when it's awkward.`,
          `One exception matters. If something false is still in motion, like a bad record headed for a jet or a commander, you stop it and report it now. The rule was never "never escalate." It's "escalate in the right order."`,
          `When this story asks what Kit should say to somebody, that's this subtest in disguise. Six competencies sit under it: Integrity and Professionalism, Leadership, Resource Management, Communication, Innovation, and Mentoring.`],
        terms: [[`Most and least effective`,`The two picks in every Situational Judgment item. Several choices can be close. You're ranking them, not hunting for the only good one.`]],
        exam: `For most effective, look for the option that deals with the person directly and in private. For least effective, look for public correction, going over a head, ignoring the problem, or a lie.` },
      { h: `Words: analogies and synonyms`,
        body: [`Verbal Analogies give you a pair and ask you to finish a second pair that matches it. Say the first pair as a sentence. A navigator measures with a sextant. Then try each answer in that same sentence. Most analogies use a short list of relationships: part to whole, tool to user, worker to workplace, degree, like warm to hot, cause and effect, opposites, synonyms, and type to category.`,
          `Word Knowledge gives you one word and five choices, and you pick the closest meaning. Twenty five words in five minutes is twelve seconds a word. Break it into pieces. Bene is good, mal is bad, chron is time, spec is look.`],
        terms: [[`Relationship`,`The link between the two words in an analogy pair, like tool to user or part to whole.`],[`Root`,`The core piece of a word that carries its meaning, like chron for time.`]],
        exam: `Watch for an answer that fits the topic but not the relationship. Navigator and crew chief are both aviation words. That doesn't make every aviation word right.` },
      { h: `The jet from outside: parts and axes`,
        body: [`The fuselage is the body. The wings make lift, with ailerons on the trailing edges for roll and flaps that add lift and drag for takeoff and landing. The tail is the empennage: a vertical stabilizer with the rudder hinged to it, and a horizontal stabilizer with the elevator.`,
          `An airplane turns around three axes that cross at the center of gravity. The longitudinal axis runs nose to tail. Turning around it is roll, and the ailerons do it. The lateral axis runs wingtip to wingtip. That's pitch, and the elevator does it. The vertical axis runs top to bottom. That's yaw, and the rudder does it. Pull back on the yoke, and the elevator's trailing edge goes up, the tail goes down, and the nose comes up. Turn the yoke left, and the left aileron goes up while the right one goes down.`,
          `Airport beacons too. White and green alternating is a lighted land airport. White and yellow is a water airport. Green, yellow and white is a heliport. A military field alternates white and green, with the white in two quick flashes.`],
        terms: [[`Empennage`,`The tail section: vertical and horizontal stabilizers, rudder, and elevator.`],[`Center of gravity`,`The point where all three axes cross.`]],
        exam: `Know each control with its axis and its motion: ailerons, longitudinal, roll. Elevator, lateral, pitch. Rudder, vertical, yaw.` },
      { h: `Tables and rates`,
        body: [`Table Reading gives you a big grid with X values across the top and Y values down the side, zero in the middle and negatives on one side. Each item names an X and a Y and asks for the number where they cross. Many of the wrong choices are the cells next door, so the whole skill is not slipping a row. Accuracy first, then speed.`,
          `Arithmetic Reasoning is word problems, 25 in 29 minutes, with scratch paper. Nobody's told me for certain whether a calculator's allowed, so practice without one. A lot of them are rates. Amount equals rate times time, so time is the amount divided by the rate. Nine thousand pounds at fifteen hundred a minute is six minutes.`],
        terms: [[`Rate`,`An amount per unit of time, like pounds a minute. Time equals amount divided by rate.`]],
        exam: `On Table Reading, X is the column across the top and Y is the row down the side. Swapping them is the most expensive mistake on the page.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f1s1`, domain:`Verbal`, task:`WK`, title:`Contingent`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Fisk came in behind her with his ears red from the cold and stood at her shoulder, reading. He was twenty, her assistant crew chief since August, and he'd come straight off the overnight at the distribution center out on Route 9. She knew that because he still had the scanner lanyard around his neck.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Pilot slot. Huh."</span> He leaned in. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"'Selection is contingent on a flight physical.' So is that like a maybe? Does the physical come before you get picked, or after?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him it means the physical can be waived for strong applicants.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fisk looks impressed. Then he reads the next line, which says nothing about waiving anything, and looks less impressed.`,
          why:`Nothing in contingent means optional. It means dependent. When a word on the test seems like it could mean what you're hoping, check it against the sentence it's in.`},
        {t:`Tell him it means you get picked first and take the physical later.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Okay, so first you get it, then they check."</span> True as far as it goes. It leaves out that the check can take it back.`,
          why:`The order's right. The meaning isn't all there. Contingent means the first thing depends on the second. Fail the physical, and the selection goes with it.`},
        {t:`Tell him it means the selection depends on passing the physical.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:2,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It depends on it. You can get picked, and if you fail the physical, you're unpicked."</span> Fisk nodded slowly. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"So it's a maybe."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"It's a yes with a string on it."</span> He liked that. He said it twice under his breath on the way out to the truck.`,
          why:`Contingent means dependent on. Something that's contingent on something else only holds if the other thing happens. The test likes words like this because people half know them. Concurrent means at the same time, and it sounds close enough to trip you at twelve seconds a word.`},
        {t:`Tell him it means the physical happens on the same day as the board.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Huh. Busy day."</span> She hears herself a second later and fixes it, but he's already headed for the door.`,
          why:`That's concurrent, at the same time. Contingent is dependent on. Two words that start the same way and mean different things are exactly what Word Knowledge likes to put side by side.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s2`, domain:`Quantitative`, task:`AR`, title:`Twelve thousand more`, q:`How long should Kit tell the expediter the whole fuel job will take?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 5:40 the expediter came on the radio. The receivers on the morning's refueling track had changed overnight, and planning wanted twelve thousand more pounds of fuel on two two eight.`,`The truck was already parked at the wingtip. Hooking up would take about six minutes. It pushed fuel at about fifteen hundred pounds a minute, and closing out and disconnecting took five more. The expediter wanted one number, and Kit worked it in her head like a chlorine feed rate at the water plant at three in the morning, before anybody could offer her a calculator.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell the expediter 19 minutes, start of hookup to disconnect.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Nineteen minutes. Six to hook up, eight pumping, five to close out."</span> A pause on the radio. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Copy nineteen."</span> It took twenty one, because the nozzle stuck, and nobody held that against her.`,
          why:`Break it into legs and add them. Twelve thousand pounds at fifteen hundred a minute is twelve thousand divided by fifteen hundred, which is eight minutes. Six plus eight plus five is nineteen. Arithmetic Reasoning is mostly this: a rate, a couple of fixed times, and one leg that's easy to forget.`},
        {t:`Tell the expediter 8 minutes, start of hookup to disconnect.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`The expediter writes down eight. At eight minutes the truck is still pumping, and the radio gets less friendly.`,
          why:`Eight is the pumping time. You were asked for the whole job. Read what the question wants before you answer the piece you just worked.`},
        {t:`Tell the expediter 14 minutes, start of hookup to disconnect.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`It runs five minutes long, and the expediter asks where the five went. They went into closing out the panel, which she skipped in her head.`,
          why:`You added the hookup and the pumping and dropped the close out. When a problem hands you three times, the answer usually uses all three.`},
        {t:`Tell the expediter 25 minutes, with some padding built in.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="man">"Twenty five? Something wrong with the truck?"</span> Now she has to explain the padding and the number she should have led with.`,
          why:`Padding isn't a sin. Giving a padded number without the real one behind it is. Know the nineteen first, then decide what margin to say out loud.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s3`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The empty outline`, q:`Kit needs that flashlight now, and a tool can't stay missing near a jet. What should she do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The engines were turning by 6:25 when the aircraft commander came on the interphone. <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Crew chief, pilot. We're showing low quantity on one of the hydraulic systems. Pressure's normal. Can you take a look?"</span>`,`Kit needed the fault isolation table out of the forms binder and a light to read it by. She knelt at her toolbox on the ramp and opened the lid. Inside was the shadow board, gray foam with a cutout for every tool she owned, and the cutout for her big flashlight was empty. Under the foam, where she never looked, were a few sheets of paper folded small, the creases gone soft from being folded so long.`,`Fisk had borrowed the flashlight for the leak check at four. She'd watched him take it. He was twenty feet off at the nose on the headset cord, hands jammed in his vest, rocking heel to toe to keep his feet awake.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Call a tool hunt on the radio and hold the launch until it turns up.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`The launch holds. Six people stop what they're doing. Thirty seconds in, Fisk finds it in his own vest and has to hold it up for six people, like a kid with a note from home.`,
          why:`A tool hunt is the right move for a tool you can't find. You could find this one with one question. Going wide before you've tried the direct fix costs time, and it costs the airman his dignity in front of a crew.`},
        {t:`Walk over to Fisk and ask him quietly if he still has the flashlight.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:2,health:3},
          after:`She walks over and stands close enough that nobody on the headset can hear her over the engines. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Fisk. My light."</span> His hand goes to his vest and comes back with it. Sleepy to sick in about a second, she'd swear later. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk" data-tone="Caught, sick, apologetic.">"Sergeant, I forgot I had it."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"I know. We'll talk after. Right now I need it."</span> The outline in the foam is full again before the interphone asks a second time.`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism, and Communication. You knew who had it, so the fastest fix was also the most private one: ask the person, face to face. If he hadn't had it, you'd stop the launch and run a tool hunt, and that would be right too. The key leans the same way on the test. Lowest level first, then go wider if that fails.`},
        {t:`Ask Fisk over the interphone, with the crew listening, where it went.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Is there a problem, Chief?"</span> There is now. Fisk answers on a hot mic with the whole crew listening, and he doesn't look at her for an hour.`,
          why:`The interphone is a public room. Correcting someone on it is correcting him in front of the crew. The test keys public correction low, and it's right to.`},
        {t:`Borrow a flashlight from the crew and sort it out after the launch.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`The jet launches. Fisk finds the flashlight in his vest while two two eight is still climbing out over the river, and goes white. For half an hour nobody could have told you where it was, and the jet was rolling the whole time.`,
          why:`A missing tool is missing until you know it isn't in the jet. Putting it off isn't patience. Here it's a risk you can't see.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s4`, domain:`Speed and Spatial`, task:`TR`, title:`Row and column`, q:`Kit has the gauge reading and the temperature. How should she pull the right step out of the table?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`She read the table on her knee with the flashlight in her teeth and the forms binder open on the toolbox lid. It was the cold weather page for exactly what the crew had: quantity low, pressure normal. The rows went down the side by the reading on the reservoir gauge. The columns went across the top by outside air temperature. Every cell held the number of the step to go to.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Fault isolation table, cold weather page (shop training copy)" say="The fault isolation table, cold weather page. Rows by the reservoir reading, columns by outside air temperature, and a step number in every cell.">Rows: reservoir reading. Columns: outside air temperature, degrees F.<br>READING · BELOW 0 · 0 TO 15 · 16 TO 32 · ABOVE 32<br>1.5 · 34 · 32 · 31 · 31<br>2.0 · 23 · 23 · 22 · 22<br>2.5 · 23 · 23 · 23 · 22<br>3.0 · 23 · 23 · 23 · 23<br>Step 22: service the reservoir to full.<br>Step 23: reading is normal for the temperature. Do not service. Recheck after warm up.<br>Step 32: check the system for a leak.</prop>`,`The gauge on the reservoir read 2.0. The thermometer bolted to the hangar wall read 8 degrees.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Go with step 22, the one she remembers from a cold morning last winter.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-4},
          after:`Step 22 is servicing. She'd have topped off a cold reservoir, and once the fluid warmed up and grew, it would've had nowhere to go but out the vent and onto the ramp.`,
          why:`Memory is what you check the table against, not what you use instead of it. Last winter was a different number.`},
        {t:`Count the columns over from the left edge, then read down to the cell.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`She counts, one, two, and lands on 23. Right cell. On a bigger page, in the dark, with the engines shaking the ramp, counting is how people end up one over.`,
          why:`You got there. Counting from the edge works on a small table and fails on a big one, and the test grid is big. Anchor on both headings and slide in.`},
        {t:`Read the crew the whole page and let the pilots choose a step.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="woman">"Chief, I've got a jet to fly. Which step?"</span> Fair question. It's her table.`,
          why:`Reading the table is your job. Handing the call to somebody with less information isn't sharing it. It's dropping it.`},
        {t:`Put a finger on the 2.0 row and one on the 0 to 15 column.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:4},
          after:`Left finger on 2.0, right finger on 0 to 15, slide until they touch. Step 23. She checks the cells around it, 22 to the right and 32 above, so she knows what a slip would have cost: a reservoir topped off cold, puking fluid on the ramp by noon. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Pilot, crew chief. Quantity's normal for the temperature. Step 23, no servicing. It'll come up as it warms."</span> <span class="said" data-who="woman">"Copy. Thanks, Chief."</span>`,
          why:`Table Reading is this, forty times in seven minutes. Find the row, find the column, read where they cross. The wrong answers on the test are the neighbors, one row up or one column over, because that's the slip people actually make. Two fingers beat one eye.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s5`, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`AI`, title:`Nose first`, q:`Which way should Kit tell Fisk the elevator moves when the pilot pulls back?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 7:02 she stood off the left wingtip in the dark and saluted. The aircraft commander saluted back through the side window, and two two eight rolled. Kit and Fisk stood at the edge of the ramp while it swung onto the runway and the engines went from loud to a lot louder.`,`It took most of the runway. Tankers full of gas always did, and Kit always held her breath for the last thousand feet anyway, which Boyd said was a waste of a good breath. Past the Bravo turnoff the nose came up, then the mains, and then it was a red light blinking out over the river toward Kansas.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"I know it's the elevator, Sergeant. I watch it move on every ops check. What I can't keep straight is which way it goes when they pull back."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him the elevator's trailing edge drops, and that lifts the nose.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fisk nods like that settles it. On Monday he explains it to a boom operator, who straightens him out in front of two people from the fuel shop.`,
          why:`Work it backward. Drop that trailing edge and you've handed the tail more lift, so up it goes and down goes the nose. That's a push on the yoke. On the pull it rises.`},
        {t:`Tell him one half of the elevator goes up and the other goes down.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`Fisk frowns at the tail. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"They moved together on the ops check, though."</span> He's right, and she knows it before he's done saying it.`,
          why:`Ailerons, Sergeant. They're out on the wings and they split, one up and one down, and the jet rolls. The elevator halves don't split. Watch the next ops check and you'll see them go together.`},
        {t:`Tell him the elevator's trailing edge comes up, so the tail goes down.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Trailing edge comes up. The wind leans on it and shoves the tail down. Tail down, nose up. Same as a seesaw at the park, only this one weighs close to three hundred thousand pounds."</span> Fisk tried it with his hand, wrist down, fingers up, and looked at it like it had told him a secret. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"That's pitch. Ailerons are roll, rudder's yaw. I'll quiz you Sunday."</span>`,
          why:`Pitch is the jet turning on its lateral axis. Picture a broom handle pushed through her from wingtip to wingtip, and the elevator's what twists it. I got this backwards myself at twenty two, in a classroom at Mather with the radiator banging. Pull back and the elevator's trailing edge goes up, not down. Aviation Information will ask you more than once and dress it differently every time.`},
        {t:`Tell him the pilot pulls back on the yoke, and the nose follows.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Fisk squints at her. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Right, but which way's the elevator going while it does?"</span> He isn't wrong to ask. She tells him.`,
          why:`You told him what the pilot's hands did. He asked what the tail did. The test likes that swap, so read whether it wants the thing in the cockpit or the thing hanging off the jet.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s6`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Around the truck`, q:`The jet's gone and the ramp is quiet. How should Kit deal with Fisk about the flashlight?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`By 7:06 it was in the clouds, and the ramp went quiet enough to hear the generator cart. Fisk hadn't moved. Both hands in his vest, chin down, like an airman who'd been thinking about a flashlight for a while.`,`${(s.picks||{})[2]===2?`He hadn't looked at her since the interphone.`:(s.picks||{})[2]===3?`He hadn't said a word since he found the light in his pocket.`:`He'd been bracing for this since the flashlight came out of his pocket in front of her.`} He'd come straight off a ten hour shift. At four in the morning he'd taken a tool out of her box without signing for it, and then forgotten it was on him.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Use it at the shift huddle as a tool control lesson, with no names.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:1,health:1},
          after:`She tells the huddle about a flashlight on the ramp at four in the morning. She doesn't say his name. She doesn't have to. Everybody was on the ramp.`,
          why:`The lesson's right and the room's wrong. A story about one airman told to twenty people names him whether you say it or not. Teach the shop later. Talk to him first.`},
        {t:`Take Fisk aside now and talk through the sign out with him.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:2,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Walk with me."</span> They go around the side of the truck, out of the wind. She asks what happened and lets him tell all of it: the warehouse, the ten hours, the leak check at four, the light in his pocket. Then she says, <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"You sign for it every time, even from my box, even at four. If you're too tired to remember a flashlight, I need to know before you're under a jet."</span> He nods for a while. <span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Yes, Sergeant."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Dry, gentle, offhand.">"And eat something."</span>`,
          why:`Leadership and Mentoring. Private, soon, and specific: what happened, what you expect, what you need to hear from him. You found the cause, which is fatigue and not carelessness, and the cause is the part that comes back. The key rewards this order every time. Talk to the person first. The chain is for when that doesn't work.`},
        {t:`Tell Boyd that Fisk took a tool without signing, and let him handle it.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Boyd hears it out, nods, and has a word with Fisk that afternoon. Fisk takes it fine. He also stops telling Kit when he's tired.`,
          why:`You skipped yourself. Going to the boss before you've talked to the airman is a move the test often keys least effective. It also teaches him to hide things from you.`},
        {t:`Let it go this once, and tell him not to worry about it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:-3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Fisk">"Okay. Thanks, Sergeant."</span> He means it. Next drill he borrows a speed handle out of her box the same way.`,
          why:`Kindness without an expectation is just a pass. He needed to hear the rule, and you needed to hear why he forgot it. You got neither.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s7`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`The flight chief`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Boyd found her at the corkboard at eleven, holding a coffee she'd let go cold, reading the posting for the fourth time.`,`Master Sergeant Arlen Boyd had been on the KC-135 since 1996 and on her since she was twenty one. He'd taught her to safety wire, to read a forms binder, to tell a pilot no. He was fifty one. When the wing converted to the KC-46 around 2030 he'd retire, and he'd told her more than once that the shop would be hers after him, the way some men tell their kids about the house.`,`He looked at the paper, and then at her, and something crossed his face that she knew. It was the look he got every time a board posting went up, a man paying on a debt he thought was his. She'd let him carry it ever since. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, gruff, wary.">"You're not thinking about that,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, gruff, wary.">"Are you?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Tell him no, she's just reading it, and get back to work.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Okay."</span> He doesn't believe her. Neither does she.`,
          why:`He asked you straight. Lying to your supervisor about something he'll find out anyway costs more than the truth would. The test keys dishonesty last, and it isn't close.`},
        {t:`Tell him the wing needs pilots who know the jet from the outside.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"The wing,"</span> he says, flat. He knows her too well. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Sure."</span> He goes back to his office.`,
          why:`True enough to say, and not the reason. Dressing up a want as a duty is a habit, and people who know you can hear it. On the test, honest and direct beats noble and vague.`},
        {t:`Ask him why he wants to know, and put the question back on him.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Boyd">"Because I'm your flight chief, Sergeant."</span> He waits. She doesn't answer, and he leaves her with the cold coffee.`,
          why:`Answering a question with a question is a way of not answering. It reads as defensive, and it was. Direct and calm is the move.`},
        {t:`Tell him yes, she is, and that he'll hear it from her first.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Yeah. I'm thinking about it. Whatever I decide, you'll hear it from me first."</span> Boyd looks at the paper a long time. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, heavy, honest.">"Okay,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Boyd" data-tone="Quiet, heavy, honest.">"Okay. Seven years ago I asked you to stay."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, guarded.">"I know."</span> He nods once, goes back to his office, and shuts the door, which he never does.`,
          why:`Communication, with some integrity in it. He asked a direct question, so he gets a direct answer, in private, from you. Telling him he'll hear it from you first is what the key rewards with a peer or a boss: nobody should find out from a corkboard. You didn't have to explain yourself yet. You just couldn't lie.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s8`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Page two`, q:`The posting is the only one on the board, and nobody's around. What should Kit do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 4:30 the jet was back, written up and put to bed with covers on and chocks in, and the shop was emptying out. Kit stood in front of the corkboard in her coat.`,`The posting was two pages stapled together, and she hadn't lifted the first one until now. The second page was the minimum scores and the packet instructions: who signed, in what order, due when. At the bottom, in smaller type, it said that routing begins with the applicant's immediate supervisor. That was Boyd.`,`It was the only copy on the board. The hallway was empty.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Take it down, fold it up, and put it in her coat pocket.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`It's in her pocket by 4:31. On Monday a staff sergeant from avionics asks the orderly room if the pilot posting came down early. Nobody knows anything about it.`,
          why:`Taking down the only copy is a choice you made for everyone else who might've wanted it. The test keys that kind of quiet advantage low, and so do people when they find out.`},
        {t:`Leave it up, and take a photo of the first page on her phone.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:-1},
          after:`The phone gets the first page under the glare of the shop lights. It doesn't get the second, with the routing order and the scores. She'll be back for it.`,
          why:`Leaving it up was right. The photo only got half. The fine print is on page two, and it's the half you'll need. Get the whole document.`},
        {t:`Leave it up, and get her own copy at the orderly room.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:3},
          after:`The orderly room is closing, and the staff sergeant at the desk prints both pages without asking why. She folds them in thirds and puts them in her coat. The board copy stays where it is, over the lost gloves, for whoever else wants it.`,
          why:`Integrity and Professionalism, in a small room. That paper belongs to everybody in the wing who might want it, and your own copy cost you a walk down the hall. Read all of it, too. You almost didn't lift the first page, and the second page is the one that says who signs.`},
        {t:`Leave it up, and walk out without taking any of it.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`She goes home without it, takes the long way, and doesn't mention it to anyone.`,
          why:`Not deciding is a decision, Sergeant. You read it five times. Acting like you don't want a copy doesn't make you want it any less.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s9`, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Break room`, q:`Kemp is running down Corbin and the colonel in front of the shop. How should Kit handle it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Sunday morning it was nine degrees, and the break room smelled like popcorn somebody had burned on night shift. ${(s.picks||{})[7]===0?`Somebody had printed a fresh copy of the posting off the wing's email and pinned it up, and Kemp`:`Kemp`}, the crew chief on the jet parked next to two two eight, had the posting off the corkboard and was reading it out loud to four people.`,`<span class="said" data-who="man" data-tone="Smug, joking, playing to the room.">"Don't bother, anybody. The colonel sent Corbin Haas from avionics two years ago because everybody liked him, and he washed out. She's never doing that again. Whoever she picks, it'll be some kid out of the university with a pilot's license and a rich uncle."</span>`,`Somebody laughed. Kemp looked over at Kit${((s.picks||{})[7]===0||(s.picks||{})[7]===2)?`, and then at the folded paper in her coat pocket,`:``} and grinned.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Catch Kemp alone after the huddle and ask him to drop it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:2,health:2},
          after:`She doesn't say anything in the room. After the huddle she finds Kemp at his toolbox. <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Corbin's in the avionics shop every drill. He can hear that story from anybody. He doesn't need to hear it from us."</span> Kemp shrugs, but he isn't grinning anymore. <span class="said" data-who="man">"Yeah. Okay. Fair."</span> It doesn't come up again.`,
          why:`Communication, and a little Leadership without the stripes. A peer who says something unfair in public gets a private word, not a public one. You stood up for a man who wasn't in the room, and you didn't make Kemp defend himself in front of four people, which is the only way he'd have dug in.`},
        {t:`Laugh along with the room and say she's only curious.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`It gets a laugh. By Monday it's going around as Darrow says she's only curious, and she'll hear that version for months.`,
          why:`Going along with gossip about a man who isn't there is the easy move, and the test keys it low. It also told the room something about you that isn't true.`},
        {t:`Tell the room that Corbin's washout is his business, not theirs.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:1,health:0},
          after:`The room goes quiet. Kemp says, <span class="said" data-who="man">"Touchy,"</span> and folds the posting into a paper airplane, which gets a bigger laugh than anything she said.`,
          why:`You were right about Corbin. Saying it to the room made it a contest Kemp had to win. Same words, one on one, and he'd have had nothing to prove.`},
        {t:`Pin a note under the posting that Corbin's record is nobody's business.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`By lunch somebody has written WHO'S CORBIN under her note in grease pencil. People who'd never heard his name on Saturday know it by Monday.`,
          why:`Who was that note for? Not Kemp. He never had to answer it. It's a public correction with nobody's name on it, which is worse, and unsigned paper on a corkboard is how one remark turns into a shop story.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s10`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Science and Aviation`, task:`AI`, title:`Green and white`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The Riverside Diner sat on the county road a mile outside the main gate, a chrome box with a counter, eight booths and a lot full of pickups. Kit took the last stool at five thirty, still in her flight line coat, bringing the cold in with her.`,`The man on the next stool was seventy something and sat up straighter than she did. Corduroy jacket. Pencil behind his ear. A crossword folded down to one square of clues. He looked at her coat, and at the red remove before flight streamer on her zipper with 228 inked on it in marker. ${((s.picks||{})[7]===0||(s.picks||{})[7]===2)?`Then at the folded paper in her pocket, where the top line showed: PILOT VACANCY.`:(s.picks||{})[7]===1?`Then at her phone, face up on the counter, with the posting still on the screen.`:`Then back at his crossword, like a man who'd already read every board on base.`}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Two two eight still flying?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"You know the jet?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Proud, plain, remembering.">"I navigated her. Twenty one years, on and off. Vern Pruett."</span> He held out a hand. It was a strong hand with a class ring worn thin on one side. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Retired. I keep the heritage room on base now, Saturdays, for the eleven people a year who come in."</span>`,`Family day came up into her throat and stuck there. Back then the wing had a whole ramp of tankers with a navigator in every one, and asking a stranger at a lunch counter if he remembered one little girl from one family day felt too much like asking for something. She shook his hand instead.`,`Out the window the airport beacon turned over the field, green, then white, green, then white. Vern tipped his head at it. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"You've seen that a thousand times, Sergeant. Coming in at night, what does it tell a pilot about this field?"</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`That it's a water airport, set up for seaplanes on the river.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"You'd need yellow for that. And a braver river."</span>`,
          why:`Yellow means water. You didn't see any yellow out there.`},
        {t:`That it's a military airfield, for the Guard base on the north side.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Close. Count the white."</span> She does. One flash, not two. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Military fields double it. This is a civilian field you all borrow."</span>`,
          why:`I see why. The Guard ramp's right there. But the beacon's the airport's, not ours, and on a military field you'd see the white twice, quick, every time it came around. You saw one white flash, then green.`},
        {t:`That it's a heliport, with pads in place of a runway.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"A heliport's got three colors. Green, yellow, white. You're one short."</span>`,
          why:`Count again. Heliports throw yellow in with the green and white, and you only had two colors out that window.`},
        {t:`That it's a lighted land airport, open at night.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Lighted land airport. Green and white."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Good."</span> Not a teacher's good. More like a man ticking a line off a checklist he'd run a few hundred times. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Plenty of folks from the base say military. The military beacons do the white twice, real quick, between the greens."</span> He watched the beacon go around again. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"This is a civilian field you all borrow."</span>`,
          why:`How many colors did you count? Two. Green, then white, over and over, is an airport on land that's lit for night. Learn four patterns and these are free points. White and yellow is for seaplanes. Green, yellow and white is a heliport. Base people miss the military one, because it's green and white too. The difference is the white. It comes twice, fast, like somebody blinking.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s11`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Verbal`, task:`VA`, title:`The napkin`, q:`Which word fills Vern's blank, so both pairs share the same relationship?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`He asked about the posting, so she told him. He'd taken a much older version of the test himself in 1973, he said, on paper, in a gym that smelled like floor wax. He took the pencil from behind his ear, a yellow number two sharpened with a pocketknife, and pulled a napkin out of the holder to go with it.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"The first eight minutes are analogies. Twenty five of them. People think it's vocabulary. It's relationships."</span> He printed in square block capitals that nobody would ever need to read twice: SEXTANT IS TO NAVIGATOR AS BLANK IS TO CREW CHIEF.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Make a sentence out of the first pair. Then the same sentence with the second. Go on."</span>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Write FLIGHT LINE in the blank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's where you work. Was a sextant where I worked?"</span>`,
          why:`That's worker and workplace. The first pair is worker and tool. Make the sentence first, and the place answers drop out.`},
        {t:`Write HEADSET in the blank.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"It's a tool,"</span> Vern allowed. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"What does a sextant do?"</span> <span class="said" data-who="Kit">"Measures."</span> <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"What does a headset do?"</span> She crossed it out.`,
          why:`Close, and that's how the test gets you. A headset is a crew chief's tool, but it talks. A sextant measures. When two answers fit the loose relationship, the tighter one wins.`},
        {t:`Write TORQUE WRENCH in the blank.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:4,health:2},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit">"A navigator measures with a sextant. A crew chief measures with a torque wrench."</span> She wrote it under his capitals, smaller. Vern looked at it for a long time, for something that simple. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"That's the sentence,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Most people write airplane and can't tell me why."</span>`,
          why:`What does a sextant do? Measures. So what does a crew chief measure with? You had it before I finished asking. Verbal Analogies is a relationship test. Say the first pair as a sentence, a navigator measures with a sextant, and the second pair has to fit the same sentence. Name the relationship and the wrong answers fall away.`},
        {t:`Write AIRCRAFT in the blank.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Everybody writes that,"</span> Vern said, not unkindly. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"The navigator didn't use the airplane to measure anything. Well. Not on purpose."</span>`,
          why:`The jet is what you both worked on, not what either of you worked with. Name the relationship first, and you'll see it's the wrong kind of pair.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f1s12`, storyQuiet:true, domain:`Judgment`, task:`SJ`, title:`Tuesday`, q:`Vern is putting on his coat, and he hasn't offered anything. What should Kit do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`Vern folded the napkin once and slid it over to her. Then he went back to his crossword and filled in a word, and she understood that he wasn't going to offer.`,`She'd never asked anybody for anything that was only for her. She'd asked Boyd for training, for the shop. She'd asked the plant for nights so the drill weekends would work. She'd asked Graham to wait, more than once. After the degree. After the inspection. Every time she'd told him what it was for, and every time he'd said okay.`,`The waitress brought the check. Vern put a five on it for his coffee and reached for his coat.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Thank him for the napkin and let him go home.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:-3,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Good luck, Sergeant."</span> The door chimes behind him. The napkin's still on the counter. She takes it, and that's all she has.`,
          why:`Waiting for someone to offer puts your problem in their hands. He wasn't going to. A direct ask was the whole job here.`},
        {t:`Ask him if he'd sit down and teach her the test.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:4,health:3},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, a little exposed.">"Would you teach me? For the test."</span> It came out in the wrong order, and she let it. Vern stopped with one arm in his coat and looked at her the way he'd looked at the napkin. <span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Deadpan, brisk, hiding warmth.">"Tuesday,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Vern" data-tone="Deadpan, brisk, hiding warmth.">"Nineteen hundred. Cedar Street, the brick ranch with the dead hedge. Bring a pencil, Sergeant. I'm not lending you mine."</span>`,
          why:`Mentoring runs both ways, and it starts with somebody asking. You went to the one man at that counter who could help, and you asked for the actual thing. I'd have said yes to a clear ask from anybody. A dressed up one I'd have had to guess at, and I was too old for guessing.`},
        {t:`Ask if he'd come teach the young airmen in her shop.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"The airmen,"</span> he says, working his other arm into the coat. <span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Bring them by the heritage room some Saturday."</span> He leaves. She sits there with a napkin and a plan for twenty year olds that she didn't want.`,
          why:`You asked, which is more than most people do. But you asked for the shop, and I answered the question you asked. Say what you need. Nobody can help with a thing you won't name.`},
        {t:`Tell him she'll stop by the heritage room sometime.`, s:0, d:{trust:0,conf:-2,health:-1},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Vern">"Saturdays,"</span> he says, and goes. Sometime has a way of turning into March.`,
          why:`Sometime isn't an ask, Sergeant. It only feels like one from the inside. If you want help, name it and name a day.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f1s4`, title:`Table Reading, slow first`,
     setup:[`Here's a small grid built like the test's, only smaller. X runs across the top from minus 3 to plus 3. Y runs down the side from plus 3 at the top to minus 3 at the bottom. Find X across the top, find Y down the side, and slide in until they meet.`,`Do these slowly and get every one right. Speed comes later. On the real test you'll pick each answer from five numbers that include the cells next door.`],
     table:[[`Y \\ X`,`-3`,`-2`,`-1`,`0`,`+1`,`+2`,`+3`],[`+3`,`37`,`40`,`41`,`44`,`45`,`48`,`49`],[`+2`,`41`,`42`,`45`,`46`,`49`,`50`,`53`],[`+1`,`43`,`46`,`47`,`50`,`51`,`54`,`55`],[`0`,`47`,`48`,`51`,`52`,`55`,`56`,`59`],[`-1`,`49`,`52`,`53`,`56`,`57`,`60`,`61`],[`-2`,`53`,`54`,`57`,`58`,`61`,`62`,`65`],[`-3`,`55`,`58`,`59`,`62`,`63`,`66`,`67`]],
     fields:[{label:`X = +2, Y = -1`,answer:60,tol:0},{label:`X = -3, Y = +3`,answer:37,tol:0},{label:`X = 0, Y = -2`,answer:58,tol:0},{label:`X = +1, Y = +1`,answer:51,tol:0},{label:`X = -2, Y = -3`,answer:58,tol:0}],
     solution:[`X = +2, Y = -1 is 60. Its neighbors are 57 and 61 in the same row and 56 and 62 above and below, which is exactly the spread the test's choices use.`,`X = -3, Y = +3 is 37, the top left corner. X = 0, Y = -2 is 58. X = +1, Y = +1 is 51. X = -2, Y = -3 is 58, the same number as an earlier cell, which happens on real grids too. Read the cell, not your memory of a number.`,`If you swapped X and Y anywhere, you'd have read the wrong column and row. X is always across the top.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f1s11`, title:`Name the relationship`, intro:`Vern turned the napkin over and wrote six more pairs. Name the relationship in each one.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`PROPELLER : AIRPLANE`, choices:[`Part to whole`,`Tool to user`,`Degree`], answer:0, why:`A propeller is one part of an airplane.`},
      {prompt:`WARM : HOT`, choices:[`Opposites`,`Degree`,`Part to whole`], answer:1, why:`Hot is more of the same thing warm is. That's degree.`},
      {prompt:`STETHOSCOPE : DOCTOR`, choices:[`Worker to workplace`,`Cause and effect`,`Tool to user`], answer:2, why:`A doctor uses a stethoscope to do the job, the way a navigator uses a sextant.`},
      {prompt:`TEACHER : CLASSROOM`, choices:[`Worker to workplace`,`Tool to user`,`Synonyms`], answer:0, why:`A classroom is where a teacher works.`},
      {prompt:`SPARK : FIRE`, choices:[`Degree`,`Cause and effect`,`Type to category`], answer:1, why:`A spark can start a fire. One causes the other.`},
      {prompt:`MAPLE : TREE`, choices:[`Part to whole`,`Opposites`,`Type to category`], answer:2, why:`A maple is a kind of tree. It isn't part of one.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`CANDID: closest in meaning`, opts:[`frank`,`cautious`,`proud`,`gentle`,`clumsy`], a:0, why:`Candid means open and honest in what you say, which is frank. A candid answer holds nothing back.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`METICULOUS: closest in meaning`, opts:[`hurried`,`careful`,`generous`,`gloomy`,`curious`], a:1, why:`Meticulous means paying close attention to every detail. Careful is the closest. Hurried is nearly the opposite.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`ABATE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`increase`,`delay`,`lessen`,`explain`,`begin`], a:2, why:`To abate is to become less strong or less intense, as when a storm abates. Lessen is the match.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`CURSORY: closest in meaning`, opts:[`thorough`,`curious`,`polite`,`hasty`,`angry`], a:3, why:`Cursory means quick and not thorough, like a cursory glance at a checklist. Hasty is the match. Curious only sounds close, and thorough is the opposite. The root is the Latin for running, the same as in course.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`CONCUR: closest in meaning`, opts:[`compete`,`object`,`conclude`,`hurry`,`agree`], a:4, why:`To concur is to agree. The con root means with, and the word means running with someone else's view.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`TENACIOUS: closest in meaning`, opts:[`persistent`,`nervous`,`suspicious`,`tender`,`forgetful`], a:0, why:`Tenacious means holding on firmly and not giving up. Persistent is the closest. The ten root means hold, as in retain.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`AUSTERE: closest in meaning`, opts:[`lavish`,`plain`,`hostile`,`ancient`,`eager`], a:1, why:`Austere means severe and plain, without decoration or comfort. Lavish is the opposite.`, task:`WK`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`FINGER is to HAND as TOE is to`, opts:[`leg`,`shoe`,`foot`,`nail`,`knee`], a:2, why:`Part to whole. A finger is part of a hand, and a toe is part of a foot. A nail is part of the toe, which flips the relationship.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`WHISPER is to SHOUT as DRIZZLE is to`, opts:[`cloud`,`puddle`,`mist`,`downpour`,`umbrella`], a:3, why:`Degree. A shout is far louder than a whisper, and a downpour is far heavier than a drizzle. Mist goes the wrong way, weaker.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`PAINTER is to BRUSH as WELDER is to`, opts:[`metal`,`spark`,`shop`,`mask`,`torch`], a:4, why:`User and tool. A painter works with a brush, and a welder works with a torch. Metal is what the welder works on, and the mask is protection, not the working tool.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`Choose the pair that has the same relationship as THERMOMETER : TEMPERATURE.`, opts:[`scale : weight`,`clock : alarm`,`ruler : pencil`,`map : road`,`oven : heat`], a:0, why:`A thermometer measures temperature, and a scale measures weight. An oven makes heat, which is a different relationship.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`ARID is to DRY as FRIGID is to`, opts:[`damp`,`cold`,`mild`,`windy`,`bright`], a:1, why:`Synonyms. Arid means very dry, and frigid means very cold.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`COWARD is to BRAVERY as LIAR is to`, opts:[`secrecy`,`courage`,`honesty`,`shame`,`fear`], a:2, why:`A coward lacks bravery, and a liar lacks honesty. The relationship is a person and the quality they don't have.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`PILOT is to COCKPIT as CHEF is to`, opts:[`cookbook`,`knife`,`menu`,`kitchen`,`apron`], a:3, why:`Worker to workplace. A pilot works in a cockpit, and a chef works in a kitchen. A knife would be tool to user.`, task:`VA`, domain:`Verbal`},
    {q:`You're a new lieutenant leading a maintenance operations section. Twice this month your senior NCO has changed the duty roster you approved and posted the new one without telling you. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell your commander the NCO is undermining your authority.`,`Ask another lieutenant how they would handle it first.`,`Let it go, because the NCO knows the people better.`,`Repost your roster with a note that only you change it.`,`Meet the NCO privately to ask why and agree on a process.`], a:4, why:`Direct and private, at the lowest level. You find out why, which may be a good reason, and you agree on how changes get made from now on. Reposting with a note corrects the NCO in front of the whole section.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a new lieutenant leading a maintenance operations section. Twice this month your senior NCO has changed the duty roster you approved and posted the new one without telling you. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell your commander the NCO is undermining your authority.`,`Ask another lieutenant how they would handle it first.`,`Let it go, because the NCO knows the people better.`,`Repost your roster with a note that only you change it.`,`Meet the NCO privately to ask why and agree on a process.`], a:0, why:`Going over the NCO's head before you've said a word to them is the classic least effective pick. It tells your commander you can't handle your own section, and it tells the NCO you'll go around them.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant in charge of a flight. Two airmen ask for the same week of leave over the holidays, and the mission lets you release only one of them. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Approve whichever request reached you first.`,`Meet with both, explain the limit, and ask if either can shift.`,`Ask your flight chief to pick one so you stay out of it.`,`Approve the airman with more leave saved up this year.`,`Approve both and tell the flight to work short that week.`], a:1, why:`Resource Management with Communication. You bring both people into the problem, say what the mission needs, and give them a chance to solve it before you rule. If nobody can shift, you decide and explain why.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant in charge of a flight. Two airmen ask for the same week of leave over the holidays, and the mission lets you release only one of them. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Approve whichever request reached you first.`,`Meet with both, explain the limit, and ask if either can shift.`,`Ask your flight chief to pick one so you stay out of it.`,`Approve the airman with more leave saved up this year.`,`Approve both and tell the flight to work short that week.`], a:4, why:`Approving both ignores the one fact you started with: the mission can't spare two. Everyone else pays for it, and you've dodged a hard talk by handing the cost to the flight.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant supervising a shop. A new airman tells you privately that a staff sergeant keeps giving them cleanup jobs and none of the training tasks they need to upgrade. Which action is MOST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the airman everyone pays dues and to wait it out.`,`Announce at roll call that training tasks will rotate.`,`Talk with the staff sergeant privately and set a plan.`,`Sign off the upgrade tasks before the training is done.`,`Report the staff sergeant to the flight commander today.`], a:2, why:`Mentoring and Leadership. You go to the staff sergeant first, in private, and you leave with a plan you can check. Maybe there's a reason. Either way the airman gets trained, and nobody gets embarrassed at roll call.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`},
    {q:`You're a lieutenant supervising a shop. A new airman tells you privately that a staff sergeant keeps giving them cleanup jobs and none of the training tasks they need to upgrade. Which action is LEAST effective?`, opts:[`Tell the airman everyone pays dues and to wait it out.`,`Announce at roll call that training tasks will rotate.`,`Talk with the staff sergeant privately and set a plan.`,`Sign off the upgrade tasks before the training is done.`,`Report the staff sergeant to the flight commander today.`], a:3, why:`Signing off training that didn't happen is a false record. It puts an untrained airman on the line with a signature that says otherwise, which is an integrity failure and worse than doing nothing.`, task:`SJ`, domain:`Judgment`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>[
    `Graham had the kitchen warm and the radio on low, a basketball game nobody was winning. He was at the fridge whiteboard with a marker, moving her plant shifts around his lab nights for the week, same as every Sunday. In the corner of Saturday he'd drawn his little cartoon tanker. He always did on drill weekends.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"How was drill?"</span>`,
    `She set Vern's napkin on the counter by the mail. ${((s.picks||{})[7]===0||(s.picks||{})[7]===2)?`Then the posting, flattened out with the side of her hand.`:(s.picks||{})[7]===1?`Then her phone, the posting still open on it.`:`She didn't have the posting. She told him what it said anyway, line by line, and found she had nearly all of it by heart.`}`,
    `Graham read it standing up, the way he read lab reports, all the way to the end. He got to the birthday and she watched him do the same math she had. He taught physics. He was faster at it than she was.`,
    (s.score>=27
      ?`Most of the weekend had gone right, and she could feel it in her shoulders, the good kind of tired.`
      :`Some of the weekend had gone sideways, and she could feel that in her shoulders too.`),
    `<span class="said" data-who="Graham">"So what are you thinking?"</span>`,
    `She had an answer ready. She'd had it ready since the corkboard: the wing needed pilots who knew the jet, the board would want somebody local, it would be good for the shop. All of it was true. She'd been saying it to herself all weekend so it would be there when she needed it.`,
    `She didn't say any of it.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Kit" data-tone="Quiet, plain, a little scared.">"I want it,"</span> she said.`,
    `She'd never said it out loud before. It came out smaller than she meant it to, about the size it had been at seven, in the back of her mother's car on the way home from family day.`,
    `Graham looked at her. The game went to a commercial. He didn't answer right away. She noticed, and she'd go on noticing it for weeks.`,
    `<span class="said" data-who="Graham" data-tone="Quiet, warm, a little unsure.">"Okay,"</span> he said.`,
    `He capped the marker without writing anything. Then he hugged her, hard, the marker still in his hand, and over his shoulder she could see the whole week on the fridge, every block of it, and the little tanker in the corner of Saturday.`
  ],
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch01.mp3`, len:`about 35 minutes`},
  next:`Chain of Command`
});
